course: everyday
lesson: 69
title: Spiders, snakes and wild neighbours
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Most spiders and snakes are harmless and even helpful, but a few have strong venom, so the safe rule everywhere is: do not touch, give them space, and tell an adult.
explain: Where dangerous ones live, people are careful: in Australia they check shoes and gloves for redback and funnel-web spiders, and in North America they keep their hands out of woodpiles and dark corners where black widows and brown recluses hide.
explain: For any snake bite you call the emergency number and keep the person calm and still, because moving spreads the venom faster; sucking or cutting the bite does not work and causes harm. In Australia an adult also wraps a firm pressure bandage while help comes.
explain: Wild animals are not pets: an injured one may bite, some can carry rabies, and a bear may chase someone who runs. Experts such as wildlife rescues handle them.
explain: A girl in Montana on a family hike sees a black bear across a meadow. She stays close to her parents, does not run, and they talk calmly while backing away slowly until the bear is out of sight.
fact: Most house spiders are harmless and catch flies; you can catch one under a cup with a card and put it outside.
fact: A few Australian spiders, such as the redback and funnel-web, have strong venom; you check shoes and gloves, and if bitten, tell an adult and call 000.
fact: In North America the black widow and brown recluse can give serious bites, so you do not put your hands into woodpiles or dark corners.
fact: The idea that we swallow spiders in our sleep is a myth; spiders avoid sleeping people, who vibrate and breathe.
fact: Many Australian snakes are venomous, so if one is in the garden you keep away, keep pets inside and call an adult or a snake catcher.
fact: After any snake bite you call the emergency number and stay calm and still, because staying still slows the venom.
fact: Sucking or cutting a snake bite does not work and causes harm.
fact: A snake's skin is dry and smooth, not slimy; it looks shiny because of its scales.
fact: An injured wild animal may bite, so you do not pick it up; an adult can call a wildlife rescue.
fact: Hedgehogs get ill from milk; a shallow dish of water or meaty cat food is safe for them.
fact: Hedgehogs are night animals, so a hedgehog out in the daytime may be sick.
fact: Rabies is a deadly illness that wild animals can pass on by biting; a wild animal that walks up to people and seems tame may be sick with it.
fact: Squirrels and other wild animals can bite fingers, so you watch them but do not feed them from your hand.
fact: Bats can carry rabies in some places and must only be handled with gloves by experts.
fact: Raccoons can bite and carry rabies, so you keep away from them and keep bins closed.
fact: Running can make a bear chase you, so you stay with adults, speak calmly and back away slowly.
exercise: There is a spider in the bath. What is a kind and safe way to move it? | Catch it under a cup with a card and put it outside | Turn on the tap and wash it down the plughole quickly | Pick it up by its legs | Squash it with your hand
exercise: A family in Sydney is going to garden. Why do they shake out their gloves and shoes first? | A redback or funnel-web spider may be hiding inside | To get the dried mud and grass out of them first | To make them warm and soft before putting them on your hands | To scare away the birds
exercise: A boy in Australia is bitten by a spider and feels pain. What should happen? | Tell an adult and call 000 | Wait to see if it goes away | Squeeze the bite hard | Go to sleep and rest
exercise: Why should children in North America not put their hands into woodpiles? | Black widows or brown recluses may be hiding there | The wood is too heavy for children to lift and move | Woodpiles are full of bees | The wood might be wet
exercise: A friend says we swallow eight spiders a year in our sleep. What is true? | It is a myth; spiders avoid sleeping people | It is true for everyone who sleeps with their mouth open | It is true only for children | It is true only in summer
exercise: A snake is in the back garden in Queensland. What should you do? | Keep away, keep pets inside and call an adult | Try to catch it in a bucket and carry it away | Poke it with a stick to make it leave | Throw stones at it
exercise: Someone in Australia is bitten by a snake. What is the right first aid? | Stay still, call 000, and an adult applies a pressure bandage | Run to the nearest house as fast as possible for help | Suck out the venom | Cut the bite and squeeze it
exercise: Why should a person bitten by a snake stay still? | Staying still slows the venom from spreading | Moving about makes the bite itch much more | The snake might come back and bite again | Staying still makes the pain stop
exercise: Should you suck or cut a snake bite? | No, it does not work and causes harm | Yes, it pulls out all the venom quickly | Yes, but only if the bite is small | Only if no adult is around
exercise: What does a snake's skin feel like? | Dry and smooth | Wet and slimy | Sticky like glue | Soft and furry
exercise: You find a bird with a hurt wing in the park. What should you do? | Not pick it up; tell an adult who can call a wildlife rescue | Take it home in your pocket and look after it in your bedroom | Throw it in the air so it flies | Give it milk and bread
exercise: A hedgehog visits your garden in England. What could you leave for it? | A shallow dish of water or meaty cat food | A big bowl of cold cow's milk and some white bread | Some bread and butter | Chocolate biscuits
exercise: You see a hedgehog walking across the lawn at noon. What should you do? | Tell an adult, who can call a hedgehog rescue | Leave it, because hedgehogs love warm sunshine | Pick it up and take it to bed | Give it a bowl of milk
exercise: Squirrels at the park come close. What should you do? | Watch them but not hand-feed them | Feed them nuts from your fingers so they get to know you | Try to catch one | Chase them up the trees
exercise: A bat has flown into your bedroom. What should you do? | Not touch it; leave, shut the door and tell an adult | Catch it carefully with your bare hands | Hit it with a pillow | Keep it in a shoebox under the bed as a pet
exercise: Raccoons are tipping over the bins at night in Toronto. What should you do? | Not go near them and keep bins closed | Feed them every night so they stop | Chase them away with a stick | Try to pet the small ones
exercise: On a hike in Canada you see a bear. What should you do? | Stay with adults, speak calmly and back away slowly | Turn round and run away as fast as you possibly can | Climb the nearest small tree | Walk closer to take a photo
test: A boy in Leeds finds a big spider in his room and wants to squash it. What could he do instead? | Catch it under a glass with a card and put it outside | Leave the window shut so it cannot get food and dies | Ask the spider to leave | Vacuum it up with the lid off
test: A girl in Brisbane is bitten on the ankle by a snake while walking. Her friend wants to carry her running to the road. What is better? | Keep her still and calm, call 000, and an adult applies a pressure bandage | Let her run to the road herself, as she will get help faster that way | Cut the bite to let the venom out | Wait an hour to see if it hurts
test: In a TV film someone sucks venom out of a snake bite. Why should you not copy this? | It does not work and causes harm | It works, but only in films | It only works on small snakes with little venom | It only works if you spit fast
test: A child in Texas wants to pull firewood from the bottom of the woodpile with bare hands. What is the danger? | A venomous spider such as a black widow may be hiding there | The wood may be too cold | The pile might be home to fish | The child may get some splinters, but nothing more than that
test: Why might a fox or raccoon that seems tame and walks up to people be dangerous? | It may be sick, and some animals carry rabies | Tame animals always want food | It is lost and wants to be somebody's pet | Wild animals are friendliest when they are ill
test: A family finds a hedgehog in their Dublin garden and puts out a saucer of milk. Why is this a mistake? | Milk makes hedgehogs ill | Hedgehogs only drink juice | Milk is too cold for hedgehogs | Hedgehogs cannot reach saucers
test: A boy on a camping trip sees a bear and his little brother starts to run. Why is running dangerous? | Running can make a bear chase you | Bears are frightened of children | Running makes the bear fall asleep | Bears cannot see people who walk
test: A girl says snakes are slimy, so she does not want to touch the class corn snake. What is true? | Snakes have dry, smooth skin that looks shiny because of scales | Snakes are covered in a thick slime that helps them to swim fast | Snakes are sticky like frogs | Snakes are wet only in winter
test: A bat is found on the floor of a classroom in Vermont. What should the teacher and children do? | Keep away and let an expert with gloves deal with it | Pick it up in a tissue and put it outside on the grass | Feed it some fruit | Keep it in a box for a class pet
test: spider in my room, gonna squish it | Catch it under a cup with a card, put it outside | Squish it, all spiders are dangerous n bite | Spray water on it till it leaves | Leave it, it will crawl in your mouth at night
test: theres a snake by the shed!! we're in Queensland | Keep away, keep pets in, call an adult or snake catcher | Catch it with a stick and throw it | Poke it so it slithers away | Go closer to see what kind it is
test: snake bit my brother, tv says suck the poison out | No, call the emergency number and keep him calm and still | Yes, suck it out as fast as you can | Cut the bite so the poison drains out | Make him run to the nearest house for help, quick as he can
test: hedgehog in the garden, can i give it some milk | No, milk makes them ill, give water or cat food | Yes, hedgehogs love a bowl of milk, it's their fave | Yes, but warm the milk a bit | Only give it milk with bread
explain: A boy in New South Wales put on his trainers without shaking them out; a funnel-web spider inside bit his foot, and he needed an ambulance and antivenom in hospital before he was well again.
explain: A teenager in Arizona picked up a rattlesnake to show his friends; it bit his hand, and he spent days in hospital while doctors treated the swelling.
fact: Most snake bites happen when people try to catch, kill or pick up a snake, not when they leave it alone.
fact: Antivenom given quickly in hospital saves almost everyone bitten by a dangerous snake or spider, which is why calling for help at once matters.
test: mate picked up a snake on the trail to show off and it bit him, what now | Keep him still and calm and call the emergency number | Tell him to walk it off on the way home, it'll wear off | Catch the snake to see if it bit hard | Put ice on it and go on with the hike
test: why do most ppl get bitten by snakes anyway | They try to grab, kill or move the snake | Snakes hunt people on purpose | Snakes chase anyone who walks past them on a path | Snakes bite whenever they get hungry
test: forgot to shake my boots out, somethin bit my toe, we're in Sydney | Tell an adult now and call 000 | Wait till tomorrow and see how it feels | Squeeze the bite to push it out | Pop the boots back on and keep going
test: did u hear, a kid at camp got bit by a snake and they reckon his whole leg went black and fell off?? what's the real lesson | Leave snakes alone, and after a bite keep still and call for help | Snakes chase kids, so never go camping at all | Always kill any snake you see near a camp, just to be safe | Suck the bite hard before the poison spreads
test: my cousin swears there's like a million funnel-webs in every shoe in Sydney, true? | Probably exaggerated, but shaking out shoes is still a good habit | Yes, every shoe in Sydney has spiders in it | No, funnel-webs live only in zoos | Yes, so nobody in Sydney wears shoes
test: everyone's saying Sam got bit by a spider cos he's dirty and gross, should i pass it on | No, it's unkind gossip; spiders bite anyone by chance | Yes, everyone should know he is dirty | Yes, but only to your close friends | Yes, if you add that it served him right
exam: Identify the principal reason for which a person who has sustained a snake bite ought to remain motionless pending the arrival of assistance. | Immobility retards the dissemination of venom through the body | Immobility deters the serpent from striking a second time upon the person | Immobility causes the venom to leave through the skin | Immobility renders the bite entirely painless at once
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the practice of sucking or incising a snake bite? | It is ineffective and is liable to cause additional harm | It is effective provided that it is performed without any delay | It is effective solely in respect of small serpents | It is recommended where no adult is in attendance
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the assertion that persons swallow spiders whilst asleep. | It is a myth, since spiders avoid sleeping persons | It is accurate, at a rate of eight spiders annually | It is accurate solely in respect of young children | It is accurate solely during the summer months
exam: Whereas a black bear is observed in the course of a family excursion on foot, determine the conduct most appropriate for a child. | Remain with the adults, speak calmly and withdraw slowly | Run away from the animal as swiftly as possible | Ascend the nearest small tree without delay | Approach the animal so as to obtain a photograph
